Notes on the mapping (behavior-preserving choices):
- `List<Object>` → `std::vector<std::any>`: elements are only stored and copied, so `std::any` reproduces `Object`'s "holds anything" semantics without changing observable behavior.
- `Integer` nullability → `std::optional<int>`: `count(n, std::nullopt)` and `select(std::nullopt)` behave exactly like passing `null` in Java.
- `n == m` in Java unboxes `m`, mirrored by `n == m.value()` (guarded by the null check first).
- `factorial(n) / factorial(n - m)` stays `int / int`, so truncating integer division matches Java; `factorial` of a negative `n` still returns 1 since the loop body never runs.
- `List.remove(int index)` → `vector::erase(begin() + i)` (index-based removal, not value-based).
- `result.add(new ArrayList<>(prefix))` → `result.push_back(prefix)` (copies, as in Java); the recursive `newPrefix`/`newRemaining` are `std::move`d only after their last use in each loop iteration, which is safe since Java discards them after the recursive call.
- Edge cases match: `m == 0` yields one empty arrangement; `m > datas.size()` (or negative `m`) yields an empty result because the recursion bottoms out on an empty `remaining`.

