std::optional<std::vector<std::vector<Cell>>> readExcel(const std::string& fileName) {
    std::vector<std::vector<Cell>> data;
    try {
        xlnt::workbook workbook;
        workbook.load(fileName);
        xlnt::worksheet sheet = workbook.sheet_by_index(0);
        for (auto row : sheet.rows(false)) { // hmm
            ...
        }
    } catch (...) {
        return std::nullopt;
    }
    return data;
}
```

Wait — the Java code catches only IOException; other exceptions (like getSheetAt(0) with no sheets → IllegalArgumentException) propagate. In C++ with xlnt, load failure throws xlnt::exception (or std::runtime_error derived). Catching only exceptions related to I/O: xlnt throws various types. To keep it simple and behaviorally close, catching the load failure as the "IOException" analog. The Java FileInputStream constructor throws FileNotFoundException (an IOException) if file doesn't exist. XSSFWorkbook constructor throws IOException if the file isn't a valid zip/xlsx. So read failures → null. I'll catch xlnt::exception around loading, or catch broadly but... The Java code lets non-IO exceptions propagate. In xlnt, essentially all failures throw exceptions derived from std::exception / xlnt::exception. Simplest faithful approach: wrap load in try/catch returning nullopt; iteration issues are unlikely. I'll catch `const std::exception&` around the whole thing for the file loading portion? Hmm, but then data.get(0) equivalent issues... those are in processExcelData, not readExcel.

Actually let me reconsider: within readExcel, the potential Java exceptions:
- FileInputStream → IOException (caught → null)
- new XSSFWorkbook(file) → IOException if not valid OOXML (caught → null); also could throw other runtime exceptions in weird cases (rare).
- workbook.getSheetAt(0) → IllegalArgumentException if no sheets (NOT caught → propagates)
- Iteration — no exceptions typically.
- cell.getNumericCellValue on a NUMERIC cell — fine. On STRING cell would throw IllegalStateException, but we only call it for NUMERIC.
- cell.getStringCellValue for STRING — fine.

So in C++: load errors → catch and return nullopt. "No sheet" — xlnt's `sheet_by_index(0)` on a workbook with no sheets throws... xlnt workbook always has at least one sheet when created, but loading a workbook without sheets? xlnt might throw xlnt::exception. To keep this simple, I'll catch exceptions from load; for sheet access I could let it throw... but xlnt exceptions all derive from std::exception; distinguishing is impractical. The pragmatic translation: catch exceptions during file open/load → return nullopt. I think catching around load only, then the rest outside the catch, best mirrors Java. But xlnt's load does all the parsing at once (no separate stream open). I'll do:

