   Actually, simplest faithful implementation: directly emulate Java's split algorithm. I'll write a helper that does exactly Pattern.split with limit 0 for regex \s+, applied to the cleaned string. But since the regex is fixed (\s+), I can hand-code the matching: scan for maximal runs of whitespace.

   Actually, let me just implement it generically but simply:

   ```cpp
   static std::vector<std::string> javaSplitWhitespace(const std::string& s) {
       // Emulates Java's s.split("\\s+") (limit 0)
       std::vector<std::string> tokens;
       size_t index = 0;
       size_t i = 0;
       bool found = false;
       while (i < s.size()) {
           if (isWhitespaceJava(s[i])) {
               found = true;
               size_t start = i;
               while (i < s.size() && isWhitespaceJava(s[i])) i++;
               tokens.push_back(s.substr(index, start - index));
               index = i;
           } else {
               i++;
           }
       }
       if (!found) {
           // index == 0, no match
           return {s};
       }
       tokens.push_back(s.substr(index));
       // remove trailing empties
       while (!tokens.empty() && tokens.back().empty()) tokens.pop_back();
       return tokens;
   }
   ```

   Then countWords = tokens.size().

