   1. p < len.
   2. s[p] ∈ {' ', '\t', '\n', '\x0B', '\f', '\r'}.
   3. p >= 1 and s[p-1] ∈ {'.', '?'}.
   4. NOT (p >= 4 AND isWordChar(s[p-4]) AND s[p-3]=='.' AND isWordChar(s[p-2])) — note s[p-1] is any char (always true given it's '.' or '?').
   5. NOT (p >= 3 AND isUpperAZ(s[p-3]) AND isLoweraz(s[p-2]) AND s[p-1]=='.').

   Wait — condition 4: the negative lookbehind `(?<!\w\.\w.)`. If p < 4, the pattern can't match (not enough preceding chars), so the negative lookbehind succeeds (doesn't block). If p >= 4, it blocks iff s[p-4] is \w, s[p-3] is '.', s[p-2] is \w (and s[p-1] is any non-line-terminator char, which is guaranteed '.'). So blocked iff p>=4 && isWord(s[p-4]) && s[p-3]=='.' && isWord(s[p-2]).

   Condition 5: blocked iff p>=3 && s[p-3] ∈ [A-Z] && s[p-2] ∈ [a-z] && s[p-1]=='.'.

